nodecode-model-router is a rewrite of Pinutss's Pinutss/jev-model-router:

  https://github.com/Pinutss/jev-model-router
  commit b977dc3ef07738aab005c433d08c1da3fa949e8d
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Pinuts (LICENSE)

The rewritten part is the routing itself: the multi-vendor catalog of the
original's catalog.py (OpenRouter, OpenAI, Groq, Together, Fireworks,
Mistral, Ollama, the same model ids and the same quality, cost and latency
numbers, with each vendor's address and the name of the environment
variable that holds its key), and the decision of router.py - the stopword
tokenizer and the lexical relevance score, the four strategies, the hard
filters in their original order (scope, disabled, already_failed,
provider, pin, capabilities, quality, cost, latency, context, key), the
deterministic ordering with its tie on the id, the select / fallback /
abstain decisions with the original's reason codes, the one-hop fallback
that honours a row's own fallback_id, the duplicate_input and
max_candidates rejections, and the per-row reasons the answer carries.
The original's MCP tool model_route is the cell's tool of the same name,
with the same arguments, and its answer is the same JSON shape the
original's to_dict wrote. Keys stay in the environment: a row names the
variable and the answer says whether it is set, never what it holds.

The optional judge is the original's provider=jev half, narrowed: one
decision endpoint (judge_url) posted {task, models} and answered
{scores:[{id, score}]}, blended at a configurable weight, with the
original's redaction.py patterns applied to the task first, and a judge
that is down losing its opinion rather than the call - where the original
raised the provider error out of the whole request. The original queried a
JEV API and an OpenAI-compatible gateway both, at 0.4 each; the cell has
one endpoint and one weight, and which vendor the caller then calls is the
row the decision names.

What was left out is the packaging it was written for: the MCP stdio
server, its framing and its four methods (a Nodecode cell registers its
tool and the organism serves it), the HTTP server with /v1/route,
/healthz and /v1/llms, the jev-model command line with demo, benchmark,
serve and llms, the JSON-file and JEV_LLM_<NAME>_* environment overlays
(the cell's catalog_file and its own per-vendor key variable cover both),
the demo models registry in scope=demo, and the HTML demo page. The
four-strategy ranking, the constraints and the abstention are the same;
the benchmark's annotated corpus is not carried over, and a row's
numbers are still declarations rather than measurements.
